/vendor/
/var/cache/
/var/*.log
.env
*.bak
*.tmp
var/locks/

# Visitor PII. This must never reach a git remote: a name, an email and a
# message replicated to a remote with no retention limit and no erasure path is
# the GDPR problem this directory layout exists to avoid. Backed up separately,
# encrypted, 30 days.
var/submissions/

# content/ — pages, uploads, revisions — is the client's data, not the site's
# code, and the two change at different rhythms once a site is live. Tracked
# here, a `git pull` over a live site overwrites what the client saved — data
# loss; ignored, a fresh clone merely has no pages until they are copied down.
# The default takes the harmless failure.
#
# Backing content up is the server's job by default. For an hourly off-box
# recovery point instead, run `git init` *inside* content/ (this ignore makes
# the nested repository invisible to the code repo), add a private remote, and
# schedule bin/backup — pages and uploads commit together, so one clone of that
# repository restores the site whole, images included. See the engine README,
# "Where content lives".
/content/
